H('ABSTRACT')
P('Relational database performance depends on how a declarative SQL statement is translated into a physical execution plan. Poorly shaped queries, unnecessary intermediate rows, unsuitable join order, and ineffective indexes can increase latency and resource use. This project specifies a compiler-driven system that analyzes SQL, represents it as a logical plan, applies semantics-preserving transformations, generates candidate variants, and evaluates them against a target database using measured execution evidence.')
P('The proposed pipeline comprises SQL parsing and validation, logical-plan construction, rule-based rewriting, candidate generation, safe execution-plan inspection, controlled benchmarking, and recommendation reporting. Candidate rewrites include predicate pushdown, projection pruning, simplification of redundant expressions, and join-order alternatives where relational semantics permit. The system retains the original query, checks result equivalence on controlled data, and compares latency and resource indicators under repeatable conditions. Database-native EXPLAIN facilities provide plan visibility; execution timing remains an empirical measurement, not an inference from estimated cost alone.')
P('The intended output is a prototype that explains transformations and ranks validated alternatives for a defined workload. This report presents the engineering design, interfaces, requirements, implementation plan, test strategy, project risks, and evaluation protocol. No implementation measurements or institutional project metadata were supplied, so this report does not claim measured speedups. The results section defines the evidence to collect before submission. The system is bounded to a selected SQL dialect and test database, with correctness, workload representativeness, and repeatability treated as primary constraints.')
P('Keywords: SQL optimization, query compiler, relational algebra, execution plan, benchmarking, database performance.');page()
