# Ch 1
H('CHAPTER 1  INTRODUCTION AND ENGINEERING PROBLEM')
H('1.1 Background',2);P('SQL lets an application describe the rows and columns it needs while leaving the database engine to choose access methods and operator order. A DBMS typically parses a statement, checks names and types, builds a logical representation, explores physical alternatives, estimates their costs, and executes a selected plan. Performance depends on semantics, data distribution, schema design, optimizer statistics, storage, and concurrent workload conditions.')
P('A compiler-oriented performance assistant can make this process more observable. It can parse a query into an intermediate representation, apply transformations only when equivalence conditions hold, and compare alternatives using the target engine. The database optimizer remains the authority for physical execution; the proposed compiler layer focuses on query analysis, candidate construction, validation, and evidence-backed recommendations.')
H('1.2 Need for the Project',2);P('Developers and database administrators often face slow queries without a direct explanation of which change is safe or useful. Manual tuning takes time, and apparently simpler SQL can change null handling, duplicates, ordering, or result cardinality. A useful tool combines plan inspection with semantic checking and controlled measurement.')
bullets(['Long-running statements degrade response time and increase compute or I/O demand.','Plan estimates can diverge from observed execution when statistics or data distributions are imperfect.','A rewrite valid for one dialect or schema may be invalid or harmful in another.','Measurements depend on cache state, concurrency, parameters, and database configuration.'])
H('1.3 Problem Statement',2);P('Given a SQL query, target relational database, and representative test dataset, design a system that generates a bounded set of semantics-preserving alternatives, validates their outputs, measures execution under repeatable conditions, and presents supported recommendations with plan and measurement evidence. It must avoid unsafe mutations, preserve the original query, and report when equivalence or improvement cannot be established.')
H('1.4 Project Aim',2);P('Design and prototype a compiler-driven system that analyzes SQL queries and identifies validated opportunities to improve query performance on a selected relational database platform.')
H('1.5 Project Objectives',2)
for x in ['Define a supported SQL dialect, workload boundary, and safety model.','Build a parser-backed representation of tables, predicates, projections, joins, aggregates, and subqueries.','Implement equivalence-guarded rewrite rules and candidate ranking.','Integrate plan inspection and controlled execution measurement with a target DBMS.','Validate result equivalence and report latency and plan evidence.','Evaluate correctness, runtime overhead, and performance across a declared benchmark set.']:d.add_paragraph(x,style='List Number')
H('1.6 Scope',2);P('Included: read-only SELECT analysis; one declared SQL dialect; a fixed benchmark schema and dataset; explain-plan capture; bounded candidate generation; result comparison; and measured observations. Excluded initially: automatic production replacement, automatic index creation, writes or DDL, multi-engine routing, and guarantees for unsupported dialects. Assumptions include a restorable test database, permission to inspect plans, and representative parameters. Boundaries include data volume, schema statistics, hardware, isolation level, and workload concurrency.')
H('1.7 Expected Engineering Outcome',2);P('A prototype software tool and reproducible evaluation package. For each query, retain source SQL, transformations, selected plan, environment, timing protocol, and whether candidates passed equivalence validation.')
# Ch2
H('CHAPTER 2  LITERATURE REVIEW AND EXISTING SOLUTIONS')
H('2.1 Review Method',2);P('The review covers foundational query optimization, DBMS plan inspection, and extensible relational planning frameworks. Primary research and official project documentation were prioritized. These sources guide the design; they do not establish results for this prototype.')
H('2.2 Review of Existing Work',2)
P('Selinger et al. describe cost-based access path selection for System R, a practical framework for enumerating plans and comparing estimated costs [1]. It shows why optimizers consider access methods and join alternatives, and why statistics and search-space limits matter. This project adopts bounded candidate reasoning but relies on the target DBMS for physical plan generation.')
P('PostgreSQL documentation describes EXPLAIN output as a plan tree with node types and estimated costs, and distinguishes plan estimates from actual execution details available through EXPLAIN ANALYZE [2]. The proposed system treats explain output as diagnostic evidence and actual elapsed time as a separate measurement.')
P('Apache Calcite provides SQL parsing, relational expressions, planner rules, and cost-based optimization [3]. Its modular architecture informs this compiler pipeline. Integrating a mature planner can reduce parser work, but dialect compatibility must be tested.')
P('Research on learned cost models explores machine-learning estimates. These models need representative training data and careful evaluation; prediction accuracy alone does not guarantee better end-to-end plans [4]. The initial prototype therefore favors explainable rules and direct measurement.')
H('2.3 Comparative Analysis',2);table(['Approach','Advantages','Limitations','Relevance'],[['Native DBMS optimizer','Uses engine metadata and operators','Opaque choices; estimates can mispredict','Plan source and inspection'],['Manual tuning','Uses domain knowledge','Slow and hard to reproduce','Baseline practice'],['Rule-based rewriter','Explainable bounded changes','Preconditions essential; gains uncertain','Core proposed layer'],['Learned cost model','May capture workload patterns','Training and drift risks','Possible future extension'],['Relational framework','Modular parsing and algebraic rules','Integration and dialect complexity','Candidate design reference']])
H('2.4 Research / Engineering Gap',2);P('The engineering gap is an auditable workflow between identifying a possible rewrite and recommending it for a particular workload. The proposed developer-facing prototype connects bounded candidate generation, explicit equivalence checks, repeatable benchmarking, and a record of transformation rationale. It is not claimed to be a new general-purpose optimizer.')
H('2.5 Proposed Contribution',2);P('A small transparent optimization assistant that gates recommendations on correctness and evidence. It connects parser-level transformations to DBMS plan inspection, retains baseline-versus-candidate comparisons, and discloses uncertainty when workload coverage is limited.')
# Ch3
