# Ch5
H('CHAPTER 5  CONCLUSION AND REFLECTION');H('5.1 Conclusion',2);P('This report defines a compiler-driven workflow for SQL performance analysis that combines parsing, logical representation, guarded rewrites, result-equivalence validation, native plan inspection, and repeatable benchmarking. A narrow initial scope supports safe and transparent evaluation. Recommendations should rely on both semantic evidence and target-environment measurements: syntax alone does not prove an improvement, and estimated cost is not runtime.')
H('5.2 Limitations',2);bullets(['One dialect cannot guarantee behavior for untested syntax or engines.','Equivalence on a test dataset is evidence, not proof for every possible database state.','Measurements vary with cache, workload, parameters, and configuration.','A small rule set may miss useful alternatives or duplicate native optimizer behavior.','No quantitative outcome is supportable until implementation and tests are run.'])
H('5.3 Future Work',2);P('Possible extensions include broader dialects, workload-level analysis, automated test generation, richer plan normalization, confidence-aware ranking, and evaluated learned estimates. Production integration needs workload governance, resource isolation, access control, rollback, and human approval.')
H('5.4 Reflection',2);P('The project balances search breadth against correctness, effort, and measurement cost. The key engineering practice is to make assumptions explicit and withhold recommendations when evidence is insufficient. The final submission should replace planning language with actual implementation, test results, lessons, and an honest objective-versus-outcome comparison.')
